questions.question_text:
Consider the graph below with $5$ vertices and $4$ edges.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,2);
\coordinate (B) at (1.5,3.2);
\coordinate (C) at (3,2);
\coordinate (D) at (4.5,3.2);
\coordinate (E) at (4.5,0.8);
\draw (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (A);
\draw (D) -- (E);
\fill (A) circle (2.5pt) node[left] {$A$};
\fill (B) circle (2.5pt) node[above] {$B$};
\fill (C) circle (2.5pt) node[right] {$C$};
\fill (D) circle (2.5pt) node[above] {$D$};
\fill (E) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$E$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Why is this graph NOT a tree?

questions.solution_text:
1. **Count the total number of vertices $n$ and calculate the required edges $n - 1$**
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,1.5);
\coordinate (B) at (1.8,3.0);
\coordinate (C) at (4.2,3.0);
\coordinate (D) at (6.0,1.5);
\coordinate (E) at (4.2,0);
\coordinate (F) at (1.8,0);
\coordinate (G) at (3.0,1.5);
\draw (A) -- (B);
\draw (B) -- (C);
\draw (C) -- (D);
\draw (D) -- (E);
\draw (E) -- (F);
\draw (F) -- (A);
\draw (A) -- (G);
\draw (B) -- (G);
\draw (C) -- (G);
\draw (D) -- (G);
\draw (E) -- (G);
\fill (A) circle (2.5pt) node[left] {$A$};
\fill (B) circle (2.5pt) node[above] {$B$};
\fill (C) circle (2.5pt) node[above] {$C$};
\fill (D) circle (2.5pt) node[right] {$D$};
\fill (E) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$E$};
\fill (F) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$F$};
\fill (G) circle (2.5pt) node[below right] {$G$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$n = 7$
$\text{Required edges} = 7 - 1$
$= 6$
$\text{Total edges} = 11$
2. **Identify and remove loops, multiple edges, and cycle edges without disconnecting any vertices**
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,1.5);
\coordinate (B) at (1.8,3.0);
\coordinate (C) at (4.2,3.0);
\coordinate (D) at (6.0,1.5);
\coordinate (E) at (4.2,0);
