\fill (D) circle (2.5pt) node[right] {$D$};
\fill (E) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$E$};
\fill (F) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$F$};
\fill (G) circle (2.5pt) node[below right] {$G$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$\text{Edges to remove} = 11 - 6$
$= 5$

questions.question_text:
The network diagram has $5$ vertices $A, B, C, D, E$ with $6$ edges.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,2);
\coordinate (B) at (1.8,3.2);
\coordinate (C) at (3.6,2);
\coordinate (D) at (3.6,0);
\coordinate (E) at (0,0);
\draw (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (D) -- (E) -- (A);
\draw (A) -- (C);
\fill (A) circle (2.5pt) node[left] {$A$};
\fill (B) circle (2.5pt) node[above] {$B$};
\fill (C) circle (2.5pt) node[right] {$C$};
\fill (D) circle (2.5pt) node[below right] {$D$};
\fill (E) circle (2.5pt) node[below left] {$E$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Which pair of edges can be removed to leave a valid spanning tree?

questions.solution_text:
1. **Count the total number of vertices $n$ and calculate the required edges $n - 1$**
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,2);
\coordinate (B) at (1.8,3.2);
\coordinate (C) at (3.6,2);
\coordinate (D) at (3.6,0);
\coordinate (E) at (0,0);
\draw (A) -- (B);
\draw (B) -- (C);
\draw (C) -- (D);
\draw (D) -- (E);
\draw (E) -- (A);
\draw (A) -- (C);
\fill (A) circle (2.5pt) node[left] {$A$};
\fill (B) circle (2.5pt) node[above] {$B$};
\fill (C) circle (2.5pt) node[right] {$C$};
\fill (D) circle (2.5pt) node[below right] {$D$};
\fill (E) circle (2.5pt) node[below left] {$E$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$n = 5$
$\text{Required edges} = 5 - 1$
$= 4$
$\text{Total edges} = 6$
$\text{Edges to remove} = 6 - 4$
$= 2$
2. **Identify and remove loops, multiple edges, and cycle edges without disconnecting any vertices**
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,2);
\coordinate (B) at (1.8,3.2);
\coordinate (C) at (3.6,2);
\coordinate (D) at (3.6,0);
\coordinate (E) at (0,0);
\draw[line width=1.6pt] (A) -- (B);
\draw[line width=1.6pt] (B) -- (C);
\draw (C) -- (D);
\draw[line width=1.6pt] (D) -- (E);
\draw[line width=1.6pt] (E) -- (A);
\draw (A) -- (C);
\fill (A) circle (2.5pt) node[left] {$A$};
\fill (B) circle (2.5pt) node[above] {$B$};
\fill (C) circle (2.5pt) node[right] {$C$};
\fill (D) circle (2.5pt) node[below right] {$D$};
\fill (E) circle (2.5pt) node[below left] {$E$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Remove $AC$ from cycle $A\text{–}B\text{–}C\text{–}A$ and $CD$ from cycle $A\text{–}C\text{–}D\text{–}E\text{–}A$.
3. **Verify that all $n$ vertices remain connected with exactly $n - 1$ edges and no cycles**
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,2);
\coordinate (B) at (1.8,3.2);
\coordinate (C) at (3.6,2);
\coordinate (D) at (3.6,0);
\coordinate (E) at (0,0);
\draw[line width=1.6pt] (A) -- (B);
\draw[line width=1.6pt] (B) -- (C);
\draw (C) -- (D);
\draw[line width=1.6pt] (D) -- (E);
\draw[line width=1.6pt] (E) -- (A);
\draw (A) -- (C);
\fill (A) circle (2.5pt) node[left] {$A$};
\fill (B) circle (2.5pt) node[above] {$B$};
\fill (C) circle (2.5pt) node[right] {$C$};
\fill (D) circle (2.5pt) node[below right] {$D$};
\fill (E) circle (2.5pt) node[below left] {$E$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$\text{Remaining edges} = \{AB, BC, DE, EA\}$
The $4$ edges connect all $5$ vertices with no cycles.
$\{AC, CD\}$ produces a valid spanning tree.
